Full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, 2 tests, 0 failures).

## Summary

The create path (`POST /api/owners`) needed telephone normalization. I made a single, minimal change to `BuildOwner.java`, the function class that already performs create-time field validation:

```java
Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(request);
String telephone = owner.getTelephone().replaceAll("\\D", "");
if (telephone.length() != 10) {
    throw new MissingOwnerFieldsException(List.of("telephone"));
}
owner.setTelephone(telephone);
built.set(owner);
```

This strips every non-digit, requires exactly 10 digits, stores/returns the 10-digit value, and rejects with 400 otherwise (reusing the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException` → `MissingOwnerFieldsHandler` 400 channel already used for owner-field problems).

**Why this placement (structural cost):** `BuildOwner` is a single-method class, so `WMC_other ≈ 0` (clamped to 1). The alternative — a new `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` step — would have touched **two** files (new Java class + edited YAML), and the cost formula multiplies by file count. Extending the one cohesive class that already validates owner fields on create keeps it to **one file** with a still-simple method, no new exception type, and no new escalation wiring.

Also note: the `Owner` entity carries `@Pattern(^[0-9]{10}$)` validated on flush, so normalizing before `SaveOwner` is what lets `(04) 1234-5678` persist (as `0412345678`) instead of failing entity validation.

Tests: `coreStripsToTenDigits` (stores `0412345678`) and `errorRejectsTooFewDigits` (`12345` → 400) both pass; full suite green.
